    <bp:eCNumber rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2.3.1</bp:eCNumber>
    <bp:displayName rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ACAT1 tetramer acetylates IDH2 dimer</bp:displayName>
    <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref347" />
    <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref348" />
    <bp:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">All enzymes of the TCA cycle are highly acetylated at multiple lysine residues (reviewed in Zhao et al., 2010). The effects of this modification on enzyme activity are largely unknown. In the case of IDH2, however, acetylation by ACAT1 homotetramer at lysine 413 abolishes its activity (Chen et al., 2021), and thereby plays a major role in TCA cycle regulation.</bp:comment>
